---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="Glossary" description="Key financial terms used throughout this guide.">
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 pb-12 pt-16 sm:px-6">
    <h1>Glossary</h1>
    <p class="mb-8 text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">
      Quick definitions for terms used in this guide.
    </p>

    <dl class="space-y-6">
      <div>
        <dt class="font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100">Asset allocation</dt>
        <dd class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">
          How your portfolio is divided among different asset classes (stocks, bonds, cash). The
          primary driver of your portfolio's risk and return. <a href="/guide/4-investment-choices"
            >More &rarr;</a
          >
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div>
        <dt class="font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100">Asset location</dt>
        <dd class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">
          Placing specific investments in the most tax-efficient account type. Different from asset <em
            >allocation</em
          >.
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div>
        <dt class="font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100">Bear market</dt>
        <dd class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">
          A stock market decline of 20% or more from a recent peak. Historically occurs every 4-6
          years. <a href="/guide/5-staying-the-course">More &rarr;</a>
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div>
        <dt class="font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100">Best-interests duty</dt>
        <dd class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">
          An adviser's legal duty to put you first. In Australia, advisers giving personal advice
          must act in your best interests (Corporations Act s961B). In New Zealand, advisers must
          give priority to your interests when they conflict with their own, and treat you fairly.
          <a href="/guide/6-next-steps">More &rarr;</a>
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div>
        <dt class="font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100">Beneficiary</dt>
        <dd class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">
          The person who receives your money after you die. In Australia, a binding death benefit
          nomination tells your super fund who to pay; in New Zealand, KiwiSaver is paid out under
          your will. <a href="/guide/6-next-steps">More &rarr;</a>
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div>
        <dt class="font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100">Bond</dt>
        <dd class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">
          A loan you make to a government or corporation. Bonds pay interest and are generally less
          volatile than stocks. <a href="/guide/4-investment-choices">More &rarr;</a>
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div>
        <dt class="font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100">
          Capital gains tax (CGT) discount
        </dt>
        <dd class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">
          In Australia, residents who hold an investment for at least 12 months pay tax on only 50%
          of the gain, for gains up to 30 June 2027. From 1 July 2027 it is replaced by indexing the
          cost base for inflation. <a href="/guide/3-investment-accounts">More &rarr;</a>
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div>
        <dt class="font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100">Compound interest</dt>
        <dd class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">
          Earning returns on your returns. $10,000 growing at 8% becomes $10,800 after year one,
          then $11,664 after year two (8% of $10,800, not $10,000).
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div>
        <dt class="font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100">Concessional contributions</dt>
        <dd class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">
          Before-tax money paid into Australian super: your employer's Super Guarantee, salary
          sacrifice, and personal contributions you claim a deduction for. Taxed at 15% (2026-27) in
          the fund and capped at $32,500 (2026-27). <a href="/guide/3-investment-accounts"
            >More &rarr;</a
          >
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div>
        <dt class="font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100">Debt-to-income ratio (DTI)</dt>
        <dd class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">
          Your total debt as a multiple of your gross yearly income. Australian banks face limits on
          lending at a DTI of 6 or more (from 1 Feb 2026). NZ banks face limits on lending at a DTI
          over 6 for owner-occupiers and over 7 for investors (from 1 Jul 2024). <a
            href="/guide/2-tackle-debt">More &rarr;</a
          >
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div>
        <dt class="font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100">Diversification</dt>
        <dd class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">
          Spreading investments across many assets to reduce risk. A fund holding 3,000+ stocks is
          highly diversified; a portfolio of 5 tech stocks is not.
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div>
        <dt class="font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100">Diversified fund</dt>
        <dd class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">
          A single fund holding shares (and sometimes bonds) across many countries and companies,
          rebalanced for you. The simplest way to invest. <a href="/guide/4-investment-choices"
            >More &rarr;</a
          >
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div>
        <dt class="font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100">ETF (Exchange-Traded Fund)</dt>
        <dd class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">
          A fund that trades on a stock exchange. Most ETFs are index funds. <a
            href="/guide/4-investment-choices">More &rarr;</a
          >
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div>
        <dt class="font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100">Expense ratio (ER)</dt>
        <dd class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">
          The annual fee charged by a fund, expressed as a percentage. A 0.03% ER on $100,000 costs
          $30/year. <a href="/guide/4-investment-choices">More &rarr;</a>
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div>
        <dt class="font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100">
          FIF (foreign investment fund) rules
        </dt>
        <dd class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">
          New Zealand tax rules for foreign shares and funds you hold directly. If their total cost
          is over $50,000 (as at Oct 2026), you are usually taxed on 5% of their opening value each
          year. <a href="/guide/3-investment-accounts">More &rarr;</a>
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div>
        <dt class="font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100">Franking credits</dt>
        <dd class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">
          Your share of the tax an Australian company has already paid on the profits behind its
          dividends. You get them back as a tax offset. <a href="/guide/3-investment-accounts"
            >More &rarr;</a
          >
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div>
        <dt class="font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100">HELP debt</dt>
        <dd class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">
          An Australian government study loan. The balance is indexed each 1 June, and you repay it
          through the tax system once your income is over $69,528 (2026-27). <a
            href="/guide/2-tackle-debt">More &rarr;</a
          >
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div>
        <dt class="font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100">Index fund</dt>
        <dd class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">
          A fund that tracks a market index by owning all or most stocks in that index. Low cost,
          broadly diversified, consistently outperforms most active funds. <a
            href="/guide/4-investment-choices">More &rarr;</a
          >
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div>
        <dt class="font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100">KiwiSaver</dt>
        <dd class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">
          New Zealand's voluntary retirement savings scheme. You, your employer and the government
          contribute, and you can withdraw at 65 (as at Oct 2026) or for a first home. <a
            href="/guide/3-investment-accounts">More &rarr;</a
          >
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div>
        <dt class="font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100">
          PIE (portfolio investment entity)
        </dt>
        <dd class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">
          A New Zealand managed fund, including most KiwiSaver funds, whose returns are taxed at
          your prescribed investor rate (PIR) of 10.5%, 17.5% or 28% (as at Oct 2026). <a
            href="/guide/3-investment-accounts">More &rarr;</a
          >
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div>
        <dt class="font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100">Rebalancing</dt>
        <dd class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">
          Periodically buying and selling to return your portfolio to its target allocation. <a
            href="/guide/4-investment-choices">More &rarr;</a
          >
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div>
        <dt class="font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100">Salary sacrifice</dt>
        <dd class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">
          In Australia, having part of your pre-tax pay paid into super. It is taxed at 15%
          (2026-27) in the fund instead of at your marginal tax rate. <a
            href="/guide/3-investment-accounts">More &rarr;</a
          >
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div>
        <dt class="font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100">Savings rate</dt>
        <dd class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">
          The percentage of gross income directed toward building wealth. A key metric for financial
          progress. <a href="/guide/0-before-you-start">More &rarr;</a>
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div>
        <dt class="font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100">Superannuation (super)</dt>
        <dd class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">
          Australia's retirement savings system. Your employer must pay the Super Guarantee of 12%
          (from 1 Jul 2025) of your earnings into your fund, and you can usually access it from age
          60 (from 2024-25) once you retire. <a href="/guide/3-investment-accounts">More &rarr;</a>
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div>
        <dt class="font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100">Term deposit</dt>
        <dd class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">
          Money deposited with a bank for a fixed term at a fixed interest rate. Often used for the
          slower-access tier of an emergency fund. <a href="/guide/1-cash-cushion">More &rarr;</a>
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
